```
function checkPrime(arr) {
  for (let I = 0; I < arr.length; i++) {
    let num = arr[i];
    if (num < 2) continue; // exclude non-positive integers

    for (let j = 2; j <= Math.sqrt(num); j++) {
      if (num % j === 0) break; // if divisible by any integer, not prime
    }

    if (j > Math.sqrt(num)) {
      console.log(num); // prime number found, output to console
    }
  }
}

// Example usage:
let myArray = [2, 11, 17, 42, 23];
checkPrime(myArray);
```

This function iterates through each number in the input array, checking if it is a valid prime number. If the number is less than 2 (i.e., not a positive integer), it skips that number.

For each positive integer, it performs a basic primality test by iterating through all integers from 2 up to the square root of the number (since any factors greater than the square root would have corresponding factors less than the square root). If the number is found to be divisible by any integer during this loop, it is not prime and the loop breaks early.

If the loop completes without finding any factors, the number is prime and its value is output to the console.

Note that this implementation is not the most efficient primality test, but it should suffice for small arrays of integers.

